  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Regional Advocacy on Regressive Policies Fostering Inequality</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to address the pressing issue of regressive policies by International Financial Institutions (IFIs) like The World Bank and International Monetary Fund (IMF), which contribute to socioeconomic disparities on a regional level in Africa, fueling inequalities. As a build up to the project &apos;Enhancing National Accountability in Resource Allocations: Towards a People-Centred COVID-19 Recovery&apos;, whose research found that IFI policies imposed onto African Governments caused governments to allocate more funds from the COVID-19 Recovery Pack to corporates compared to social sectors benefiting the marginalised groups. Implications of such actions reach far and wide in terms of widening the inequality gap between the poor and rich. Having acknowledged that policies and practices of IFIs significantly influence the national, regional and global economic landscape, it is imperative in this project to tackle this negative influence from IFI policies and practices.&#xa0;
The project will therefore spearhead a comprehensive regional advocacy initiative to address and rectify the adverse impact&#xa0;of regressive IFI policies&#xa0;on socioeconomic inequality within Africa, evaluate the existing policies of major IFIs to identify instances where regressive measures contribute to regional socioeconomic disparities and collaborate with a network of stakeholders, including government officials, civil society organisations and private sector representatives to tackle policies and practices fuelling inequality in Africa.</narrative>
  </description>
